  "grid_title": "SHOULDER INJURIES",
  "why": "Dislocation: the ball of the arm bone pops out of the socket, almost always out the front. Rotator cuff tear: four muscles hold the ball in the socket; the tendon on top (supraspinatus) tears most often, from wear, a fall or lifting. Separation: landing on the point of the shoulder stretches or [tears](tˈɛɹz) the ligaments that hold the collarbone to the shoulder blade. Labral tear: the rim of cartilage around the socket tears, often from a dislocation or lots of throwing. A shoulder that popped out needs the ER (never push it back in yourself); pain that won't quit, get it checked.",
